%S A399669 1,1,0,2,2,1,2,3,2,2,3,3,2,5,3,0,6,4,3,5,5,3,5,6,3,6,7,2,8,6,1,10,6,4,
%T A399669 8,8,2,9,10,4,7,12,3,8,10,3,12,9,5,9,11,6,12,11,5,10,16,1,10,14,2,14,
%U A399669 10,6,9,13,7,13,13,4,10,17,3,14,13,3,16,11
%N A399669 Number of primes p < 2*n that do not appear in any Goldbach partition of 2*n, but appear in at least one Goldbach partition of 2*n + 2.
%C A399669 Conjecture: a(n) >= 1 for all n > 105 (i.e., for every even integer 2*n > 210, there is at least one prime p < 2*n not used in any Goldbach partition of 2*n that is used in a Goldbach partition of 2*n + 2).
%C A399669 Verified up to 2*n = 10^6.
%C A399669 Note: For every n >= 3, there is at least one prime p < 2*n unused in the Goldbach partitions of 2*n such that (2*n + 2) - p is composite (see Pham (2026)).
%H A399669 Pierre Pham, <a href="https://math.stackexchange.com/a/5154700/1786098">Proof of the complementary observation</a>, Math StackExchange, Nov 2026
%e A399669 For n = 3 (2*n = 6), the only Goldbach partition of 6 is (3, 3). The unused prime < 6 is 5, which appears in the partition (3, 5) of 8. Thus a(3) = 1.
%e A399669 For n = 5 (2*n = 10), the Goldbach partitions of 10 are (3, 7) and (5, 5). The only unused prime < 10 is 2, which does not appear in any Goldbach partition of 12 = 5 + 7. Thus a(5) = 0.
%e A399669 For n = 6 (2*n = 12), the Goldbach partition of 12 is (5, 7). The unused primes < 12 are 2, 3, 11; among these, 3 and 11 appear in the partition (3, 11) of 14. Thus a(6) = 2.
%e A399669 For n = 10 (2*n = 20), the Goldbach partitions of 20 are (3, 17) and (7, 13). The unused primes < 20 are 2, 5, 11, 19; among these, 5, 11, and 19 appear in the partitions (5, 17), (11, 11), and (3, 19) of 22. Thus a(10) = 3.
%o A399669 (PARI) a(n) = my(c=0); forprime(p=2, 2*n-1, if(!isprime(2*n-p) && isprime(2*n+2-p), c++)); c;
%o A399669 (Python)
%o A399669 from sympy import isprime, primerange
%o A399669 def a(n):
%o A399669     N = 2 * n
%o A399669     primes = list(primerange(2, N))
%o A399669     used_in_N = {p for p in primes if isprime(N - p)}
%o A399669     unused_in_N = [p for p in primes if p not in used_in_N]
%o A399669     return sum(1 for p in unused_in_N if isprime(N + 2 - p))
%o A399669 print([a(n) for n in range(3, 81)])
%Y A399669 Cf. A400895.
%K A399669 nonn,new
